This is example to build a sorted, bit-transposed dictionary vector of names of 
celestial objects from NED objects directory. 
Example shows how to do sorted search, runs benchmarks, checks self correctness. 


0. How to build

- go to the source directory of BitMagic
>cd ../..
- setup the compile root directory
as
>source ./bmenv.sh
or
>. ./bmenv.sh
- get back to the sample 
>cd -
>make
or
>./build_all.sh


1. Download the fragment of catalog from 

http://bitmagic.io/data/ned.txt.gz

or use NED ADQL interface:

curl -o ned.txt "https://ned.ipac.caltech.edu/tap/sync?query=SELECT+*+FROM+ned_objdir&LANG=ADQL&REQUEST=doQuery&FORMAT=text"

It will produce a columnar report similar to this:

      bhextin      |         dec          |       gallat       |      gallon       |            prefname            |pretype |   
-------------------|----------------------|--------------------|-------------------|--------------------------------|--------|
0.0                |61.090161208000005    |55.6336642907128    |129.592988077481   |"2MASX J12201807+6105248       "|"G     "|
0.0                |56.1873074943         |60.7497277891121    |128.223612727992   |"2MASX J12325147+5611139       "|"G     "|
0.0                |58.25105334925        |58.794082336604     |126.228935296354   |"2MASS J12382714+5815046       "|"G     "|             
0.0                |57.223830104799994    |59.8791937528706    |124.806822462432   |"2MASS J12442918+5713259       "|"*     "|


OVERFLOW (more rows were available but have been truncated by the TAP service)


NOTE: Out of this columnar report we need only "prefname" - names of objects in the NED catalog.
Example will parse out the column and create a sorted STL vector, STL map (R-B tree) and
bit transposed sparse vector (BitMagic) to compare memory footprint and search performance.



2. Run xsampl05 to load the report and build index

./xsample05 -idict ned.txt -svout astra.bm -t

Expected output:

Reading input file: 1000000
Loaded 380132 dictionary names.

Performance:
1. parse input data ; 53.74 sec
2. build sparse vector; 1.276 sec
7. Save sparse vector; 4.726 ms

See also:
./run_all_compr.sh



3. Run search benchmarks using the compressed sparse vector of "prefname"

./xsample05  -svin astra.bm -t -b


Expected output:


Picked 15000 samples. Running benchmarks.

Performance:
3. std::lower_bound() search; 427000 ops/sec; 35.08 ms
4. std::map<> search; 285000 ops/sec; 52.47 ms
5. bm::sparse_vector_scanner<> search; 21000 ops/sec; 685.2 ms
6. bm::sparse_vector_scanner<> binary search; 42000 ops/sec; 351 ms
8. Load sparse vector; 3.951 ms

See also:

./run_all_bench.sh

4. Compare serialization levels

Build xsample05 and prepare two serialized inputs from the same dictionary,
one without remapping and one with -remap. Then run:

python3 run_level_matrix.py --program ./xsample05_neon \
  --plain /path/to/plain.sv --remap /path/to/remapped.sv \
  --output /path/to/new-results-directory --repeats 3

The program path may select a scalar or SIMD build. The results directory must
not already exist. The matrix covers levels 6 through 10, with and without XOR,
for both inputs. Each run checks a memory deserialization against its source.
The CSV records serialized bytes and in-memory serialization/deserialization
milliseconds; file I/O and validation are outside these timings. The first
repetition of each combination also reports top-level bit-plane opcode counts
and bytes, excluding the sparse-vector container and digest. Individual logs
and one serialized output per combination are retained in the results directory.

5. Experimental four-candidate reference-XOR selection

-xor-candidates4 enables XOR and compares the incumbent recipe with up to three
single-reference alternatives shortlisted by the existing block-gain estimate.
Only blocks with an incumbent recipe participate; search limits, early stopping
and estimator formulas are unchanged. The shortlist is captured before greedy
chain refinement. Levels 7 and higher also enable shared reference metadata and polarity records. All complete record bytes count;
ordinary encoding wins ties, followed by the incumbent. The mode is enabled by default at levels 7 and higher when XOR is enabled.
The underlying serializer setting must be enabled before building the XOR model.

python3 run_xor_candidates.py --program ./xsample05_neon \
  --input /path/to/plain.sv --output /path/to/new-results-directory \
  --levels 6 7 8 --repeats 3

The harness alternates paired run order, checks every round trip and retains
profiles, outputs, individual timings and a median summary. Optional --reference
points to a previous NED matrix directory and requires baseline files to match it
byte for byte. XOR counters report incumbent blocks, additional trials, changed
block decisions and saved record bytes; composite metadata may change separately.

7. Experimental compact wave masks

-xor-h64 enables XOR and permits opcode 99 (bm::set_block_xor_ref8_h64).
For a masked single reference with a row ID below 256, the reference prefix
contains the opcode, row ID, an eight-bit presence mask, and the nonzero mask
bytes in low-to-high order. One through six nonzero bytes make H64 smaller.
Seven or eight use the existing fixed mask. The all-wave reference keeps its
unmasked encoding. Combine -xor-h64 with -xor-candidates4 to price this prefix
during each trial and with -profile-opcodes to count the emitted records.
Files containing opcode 99 require a reader that supports it.

8. Experimental parallel gather

-xor-gather enables four-candidate XOR, H64 and parallel gather trials at depth
16. Each target wave selects one source from the next sixteen reference rows
using the symmetric density/run score. Missing blocks consume depth. Three
complete records try current, sparse and dense residual polarity. Ordinary
or vertical XOR wins ties against gather.

Opcode 100 (bm::set_block_xor_gather) stores a flags byte, an H64 target mask
and 32-bit-padded gamma coordinates. Bit 0 adds a polarity bit after each
coordinate. Bit 1 encodes the positive difference between source and target
global plane IDs. A record with any selected source ID at or below the target
uses absolute IDs (source + 1). -xor-gather-absolute forces absolute IDs.
Older absolute-ID records decode with the same meaning.

The library's set_xor_gather(true, true) enables relative coordinates. When
decoding, the destination vector identifies the target ID in the attached
reference collection. For a separate destination, call set_gather_target_id()
with the global target ID. Target wave positions are implicit. One ordinary
residual block follows, and the decoder reconstructs before OR-ing target bits.

Experimental opcode 106 arithmetic coding is enabled by presets 7–10. At level
7 only pooled-nibble arithmetic competes when the final selection leaves a raw BIT block. At
level 8 pooled-nibble arithmetic competes for originals and XOR/gather
residuals. Levels 9–10 compare both byte and pooled-nibble models. Compare with
-no-arithmetic; -arithmetic restores it after
selecting the preset. Level 6 retains its original bytes. Opcode reports include
final mode counts for originals and embedded XOR contexts.
Experimental opcode 107 additionally tries byte and pooled-nibble arithmetic
on the copied words of opcode 22 records at levels 9–10. Levels 7–8 try only
pooled-nibble arithmetic on copied words, including XOR residuals. A histogram
admission
estimate excludes candidates unlikely to beat the complete current record.
Its decoder uses bounded virtual zero lookahead after the physical payload.
Example (unmapped input):
  ./xsample05 -svin ned_plain.sv -svout L8.sv -level 8 -xor -profile-opcodes
  ./xsample05 -svin ned_plain.sv -svout L8-off.sv -level 8 -xor -no-arithmetic

Arithmetic alphabet override:
  -arithmetic-mode nibble|byte|all
Apply this after the level policy through the underlying bvector serializer's
set_arithmetic_coding_mode(), using bm::arithmetic_coding_mode::nibble,
bm::arithmetic_coding_mode::byte or bm::arithmetic_coding_mode::all.
The presets use nibble at L7/L8 and all at L9/L10. Explicit mode overrides affect
both raw-block and copied-word arithmetic candidates, including XOR residuals.

5. Freeze and validate the compression reference matrix

Build xsample05 first. Prepare one ordinary and one remapped sparse vector from
exactly the same sorted NED names. `-svin` in the matrix consumes these prepared
vectors; `-remap` applies when the inputs are prepared, not during a matrix case.
For example, `run_all_compr.sh` can produce both inputs from a NED report.
Creation first verifies that both prepared inputs contain the same strings in the
same order.

Create all levels 0–10 with reference XOR off/on for both inputs (44 cases):

    ./create_references.sh --reference-dir /path/to/ned-references \
        --bin ./xsample05_neon --plain /path/to/ned_plain.sv \
        --remap /path/to/ned_remap.sv -j 4

Validate every frozen stream with a current xsample05 executable:

    ./validate_references.sh --reference-dir /path/to/ned-references \
        --bin ./xsample05_neon -j 4

`--levels 0,6,10` selects a staged subset. `-j` bounds simultaneous sample
processes, not their internal threads. Both commands return a nonzero status if
any case fails. The reference creator records the input and generator checksums
in matrix.json. On restart it verifies completed cases and creates missing ones.
It refuses a different generator or input in the same reference directory.
Each case contains its stream, opcode profile, creation log, validation log,
command, checksums and timing metadata. A failed or interrupted case remains
under .partial with its logs, and published case directories are never replaced.
A creator lock prevents two generators from publishing to the same directory.

Validation compares each decoded stream with its matching prepared input vector.
To use copies of the inputs at another path, pass `--plain` and `--remap` to the
validation command. Their hashes must match matrix.json. Profile counts describe
bit-plane blobs; complete stream size also includes sparse-vector framing.
